{:trait/id :trait-deu-communication-directness, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "DEU"], :trait/title "Directness valued in communication", :trait/description "Germans prioritize clear, direct communication. 'It's not bad' (understatement) is actually high praise. Indirect hints are considered confusing or dishonest.", :trait/contrast "Not a lack of politeness; it's a different politeness convention (honesty over face-saving)", :trait/source "Erin Meyer, Culture Map; Hofstede Cultural Dimensions", :trait/caveat "Individual Germans vary widely; regional (North/South), generational, and professional context matter. This is an observed tendency, not a rule. East/West differences also exist post-reunification."}
{:trait/id :trait-deu-time-monochronic, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "DEU"], :trait/title "Time as sequential resource (monochronic)", :trait/description "Germans treat time as a linear, segmented commodity. Punctuality is non-negotiable; multi-tasking is valued; schedules are firm commitments.", :trait/contrast "Not rigidity for its own sake; reflects respect for others' time and predictability in systems", :trait/source "Edward T. Hall, Beyond Culture", :trait/caveat "Varies by industry, generation, and urban/rural divide. Younger Germans may be more flexible. Corporate culture strongly reinforces monochronic norms."}
{:trait/id :trait-ita-time-polychronic, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "ITA"], :trait/title "Time as fluid, relationship-centered (polychronic)", :trait/description "Italians prioritize relationships and serendipity over strict schedules. Multiple concurrent activities are normal. Unexpected connections matter more than clock time.", :trait/contrast "Not irresponsibility; reflects valuing human flexibility and spontaneity over rigid systems", :trait/source "Edward T. Hall, Beyond Culture; Hofstede IVR (Indulgence vs Restraint)", :trait/caveat "Professional/business contexts increasingly adopt monochronic norms. Urban younger Italians may differ from rural or older generations. Business districts operate under different time culture."}
{:trait/id :trait-ita-communication-harmony, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "ITA"], :trait/title "Relationship harmony, indirect when necessary", :trait/description "Italians value warm, personal connections. Conflict is often softened through indirectness or humor. Face-saving is important.", :trait/contrast "Not deceptiveness; relationships and emotional warmth are genuine; indirect communication preserves connection quality", :trait/source "Erin Meyer, Culture Map", :trait/caveat "Generational divide: younger, urban Italians may be more direct. Business communication increasingly mirrors Northern European directness."}
{:trait/id :trait-jpn-communication-indirect, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "JPN"], :trait/title "Indirect communication to preserve group harmony", :trait/description "Japanese prioritize group cohesion and conflict avoidance. Disagreement is expressed subtly; direct refusal is rare. Context and silence carry meaning.", :trait/contrast "Not evasiveness; the goal is sustaining relationships and group stability, not deception", :trait/source "Erin Meyer, Culture Map; Edward T. Hall (high-context)", :trait/caveat "Generational change: younger Japanese, especially internationally educated, may be more direct. Corporate/business settings internationally tend toward directness."}
{:trait/id :trait-jpn-value-group-harmony, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "JPN"], :trait/title "Group harmony prioritized over individual expression", :trait/description "Japanese culture emphasizes 'wa' (harmony). Individual desires are subordinated to group needs. Consensus-building is slow but binding.", :trait/contrast "Not suppression of self; many individuals find fulfillment in collective identity and contribution", :trait/source "Hofstede Power Distance & Collectivism dimensions; Japanese psychology literature", :trait/caveat "Varies significantly by age, urban/rural, industry. Younger generations show increasing individualism. Rural and small-town contexts may maintain higher collectivism."}
{:trait/id :trait-usa-individualism, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "USA"], :trait/title "Individualism, self-reliance, egalitarian tone", :trait/description "American culture emphasizes individual achievement, personal responsibility, and informal egalitarianism. Titles and hierarchy are de-emphasized in speech.", :trait/contrast "Not lack of community; Americans also value teamwork; but framing emphasizes individual contribution and choice", :trait/source "Hofstede Individualism dimension; Pew Research surveys", :trait/caveat "Extreme variation: Southern US more collectivist; urban coasts more individualist. Class, race, and regional differences substantial. Implicit hierarchies often exist despite egalitarian language."}
{:trait/id :trait-usa-time-monochronic, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "USA"], :trait/title "Time as resource to be managed", :trait/description "Americans treat time as money. Punctuality matters; multi-tasking is normal; efficiency is valued. 'Time is of the essence' frames business interactions.", :trait/contrast "Not workaholism by necessity; reflects deep cultural assumption that time is scarce and valuable", :trait/source "Edward T. Hall, Beyond Culture", :trait/caveat "Regional and class variation: rural South more relaxed; tech industry more intense. Generational differences emerging."}
{:trait/id :trait-bra-warmth-relationships, :trait/kind :hospitality-norm, :trait/region [:region/id "BRA"], :trait/title "Physical warmth, personal connection, relatedness", :trait/description "Brazilians emphasize physical proximity, hugs, and personal warmth in relationships. Business relationships are built on personal connection first.", :trait/contrast "Not unprofessionalism; personal trust is the foundation for professional dealings", :trait/source "Erin Meyer, Culture Map; anthropological research on Brazilian culture", :trait/caveat "Urban vs rural, class, and regional differences. Favela to corporate culture varies. Younger urban professionals may adopt more Northern European norms in global contexts."}
{:trait/id :trait-bra-time-polychronic, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "BRA"], :trait/title "Time as flexible, relationship-centered", :trait/description "Brazilians view time as flexible and fluid. Relationships and immediate human needs often override schedules. Multi-tasking and interruptions are normal.", :trait/contrast "Not unreliability; reflects that human connection and adaptability are higher priorities than clock adherence", :trait/source "Edward T. Hall, Beyond Culture", :trait/caveat "Urban business districts increasingly adopt monochronic culture. International companies operate under different norms. Generational shift toward more time-consciousness occurring."}
{:trait/id :trait-fra-skepticism-authority, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "FRA"], :trait/title "Skepticism of authority, valuing intellectual critique", :trait/description "French culture encourages questioning, debate, and intellectual rigor. Authority is not assumed; arguments must be defended. Critique is a form of engagement, not rudeness.", :trait/contrast "Not cynicism or lack of respect; critique is seen as serious engagement with ideas", :trait/source "Erin Meyer, Culture Map; French philosophy tradition", :trait/caveat "Professional hierarchies are strong; intellectual debate is valued within frameworks. Regional variation: Parisian vs provincial. Generational shifts in educational culture."}
{:trait/id :trait-ind-collectivism-family, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "Strong family/group collectivism, hierarchical respect", :trait/description "Indian culture emphasizes family, caste/social group, and hierarchical relationships. Decisions are made considering family/group impact. Elders are respected.", :trait/contrast "Not lack of individual ambition; individuals pursue goals within family/group framework", :trait/source "Hofstede Power Distance & Collectivism; Indian cultural studies", :trait/caveat "Extreme diversity: India contains multiple religions, languages, castes. Urban IT professionals vs rural populations differ vastly. Generational rapid change in urban centers."}
{:trait/id :trait-nld-direct-egalitarian, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "NLD"], :trait/title "Direct communication, minimal hierarchy", :trait/description "Dutch communication is frank and explicit. Hierarchy is minimized in speech; directness is valued over politeness formalities. 'Telling it like it is' is admired.", :trait/contrast "Not rudeness; Dutch directness is pragmatic and not intended as personal attack", :trait/source "Erin Meyer, Culture Map; Hofstede (low Power Distance)", :trait/caveat "Generational and professional context matter. Academic/corporate settings may still maintain implicit hierarchies despite egalitarian speech norms."}
{:trait/id :trait-fin-quiet-reflection, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "FIN"], :trait/title "Quiet, reflective, silence comfortable", :trait/description "Finnish communication is sparse and thoughtful. Silence is acceptable and not awkward. Small talk is minimal; meaningful conversation is preferred.", :trait/contrast "Not unfriendliness; Finns value substance and authenticity over surface politeness", :trait/source "Erin Meyer, Culture Map; Finnish cultural psychology", :trait/caveat "Urban/cosmopolitan Finns may be more talkative. Generational shifts toward more expressiveness in younger cohorts."}
{:trait/id :trait-ae-hospitality-formality, :trait/kind :hospitality-norm, :trait/region [:region/id "AE"], :trait/title "Formal hospitality protocols, respect for status", :trait/description "UAE culture emphasizes formal protocols, respect for hierarchy and status, and generous hospitality. Business relationships involve status differentiation.", :trait/contrast "Not cold or impersonal; hospitality is genuine; formality is a sign of respect", :trait/source "Anthropological and business culture research on Gulf states", :trait/caveat "Rapid modernization: Dubai & Abu Dhabi have cosmopolitan, Western-influenced culture. Bedouin heritage vs urban corporate culture. Expatriate vs Emirati culture diverge."}
