{:trait/id :trait-deu-queer-island-berlin, :trait/city [:city/id "DEU-berlin"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "DEU"], :trait/title "Berlin as LGBTQ+ urban island & cultural safe harbor", :trait/description "Berlin (Kreuzberg, Schönebergneighborhoods) is Germany's most gender-variant & LGBTQ+-affirming city. History of tolerance (Weimar era, post-1989 creative influx). Pride & queer identity integrated into city brand.", :trait/contrast "NOT universal in rural Germany; strong rural/urban divide on gender/sexuality attitudes.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Pew Research EU", :trait/caveat "Right-wing backlash rising in Germany; AfD politics challenge Berlin tolerance. Gentrification threatening queer neighborhoods. Younger cohorts more global, older cohorts shaped by Cold War/division."}
{:trait/id :trait-bra-inequality-sharp-urban-zones, :trait/city [:city/id "BRA-sao-paulo"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "BRA"], :trait/title "São Paulo: extreme inequality & favela/penthouse divide", :trait/description "São Paulo (~12M metro) embodies Brazil's wealth chasm. Gated high-rises (Zona Sul) juxtapose adjacent favelas (Zona Leste/Norte). Extreme segregation shapes worldviews. Class intersects with race; wealth inequality reflects colonial/slavery legacy.", :trait/contrast "NOT fatalism; São Paulo vibrant cultural production despite/because of inequality.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "UN Habitat", :trait/caveat "Younger generations navigating identity across class/race boundaries. Favela residents show resilience & cultural agency. Gentrification & police violence affecting inequality perception."}
{:trait/id :trait-bra-warmth-physical-proximity-urban, :trait/city [:city/id "BRA-sao-paulo"], :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "BRA"], :trait/title "Brazilian warmth: physical proximity & emotional expressiveness in megacity", :trait/description "São Paulo residents, despite urban chaos, maintain warm personal connections. Embracing, touching, emotional expressivity normative even with strangers. Carnival, music, celebration integrate into daily rhythm despite poverty.", :trait/contrast "NOT naivety; warmth coexists with sharp street awareness in high-crime context.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Meyer, Shweder", :trait/caveat "Safety concerns (especially Zona Leste) reshape proximity norms for some groups. Gender affects comfort with physical proximity. Generational & class differences in expressiveness."}
{:trait/id :trait-egy-wasta-networks, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "EGY"], :trait/title "Wasta: relationship-based access, patronage networks", :trait/description "Egyptian society operates through 'wasta' (connections/influence networks). Business, government, education access depends on family/social networks. Formal rules are often bypassed through personal relationships. Trust is relationship-based, not institutional.", :trait/contrast "NOT corruption only; wasta reflects deep social structure where relationships precede formal systems.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede & Hofstede 'Cultures & Organizations'; Eckert & Chadha (2013) 'Egypt's Internal Conflicts'; business anthropology", :trait/caveat "Generational change: younger, educated Egyptians frustrate wasta opacity; online activism critiques it. Urban/rural divide: rural wasta more kinship-based; urban more professional networks. Post-2011 revolution rhetoric challenged but not eliminated patronage systems. Individual variation by social class & education high."}
{:trait/id :trait-egy-honor-hierarchy, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "EGY"], :trait/title "Honor hierarchies, formal respect, shame/honor distinction", :trait/description "Egyptian culture emphasizes family honor, formal respect for elders/authority, and shame sensitivity. Reputation matters deeply. Honor can be lost through family member's actions. Formal titles and hierarchies are respected. Public criticism is humiliating.", :trait/contrast "NOT lack of individual dignity; honor is collective family/community attribute. Different framing than Western individualistic shame.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Abu-Lughod (1986) 'Veiled Sentiments'; Patai (2002) 'Arab Culture & Society'; Hofstede Power Distance", :trait/caveat "Urban secular Egyptians increasingly distance from strict honor codes. Gender dimensions: women's honor traditionally controlled more than men's (changing). Religious identity (Muslim, Coptic, etc.) shapes honor frameworks differently. Diaspora Egyptians often retain honor-sensitivity more strongly than home-country cohort."}
{:trait/id :trait-egy-islamic-time, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "EGY"], :trait/title "Islamic & relationship-centered time: prayer rhythm, flexibility", :trait/description "Egyptian time culture integrates Islamic prayer schedules (5 daily prayers), which structure work/social rhythm. Time is also relationship-centered; human needs take precedence over clocks. Ramadan fasting shifts time perception & work schedules. Flexibility over rigidity.", :trait/contrast "NOT disorganization; rhythm follows religious practice & social bonds, not clock efficiency.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hall 'Beyond Culture' (polychronic model); Islamic practice anthropology; Hofstede IVR", :trait/caveat "Urban corporate Egypt increasingly adopts monochronic norms for business efficiency. Secular/non-Muslim minorities experience time differently. Generational shift: younger Egyptians integrate Islamic schedule with modern work demands (compromise on prayer times). Business districts operate under different time culture than residential areas."}
{:trait/id :trait-egy-oral-tradition, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "EGY"], :trait/title "Oral tradition, storytelling, collective narrative culture", :trait/description "Egyptian culture values oral storytelling, poetry, and narrative over written documentation. History is transmitted through stories, genealogy, and collective memory. Formal meetings may include narrative context-setting. Interpersonal communication emphasizes dramatic narrative.", :trait/contrast "NOT lack of written skill; written Arabic has rich literary tradition. Preference is for oral transmission & embodied storytelling.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Ong (1982) 'Orality & Literacy'; Mayerhofer (2010) 'Arab Media Studies'; anthropological observation", :trait/caveat "Highly educated, urban Egyptians proficient in written media (journalism, literature, social media). Generational shift toward written digital communication. Regional variation: rural oral tradition stronger; urban increasingly balanced with written media."}
{:trait/id :trait-mumbai-multicultural-chaos, :trait/city [:city/id "IND-mumbai"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "Multicultural normalization: multiple languages, religions, communities coexist", :trait/description "Mumbai's extreme diversity (Hindu, Muslim, Christian, Sikh, Buddhist, Jewish communities; Hindi, English, Marathi, Tamil, Telugu speakers; migrants from all of India) creates normalized coexistence & cultural fluidity. Festivals of all religions are observed. Code-switching between languages is normal.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to rural India: more religiously/linguistically homogeneous; single community often dominant.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Appadurai (1996) 'Modernity at Large'; Mehta (2004) 'An Illusion of Order: Indian Business, Society & State'; urban ethnography", :trait/caveat "Coexistence is normalized but tensions/communal violence still occur (2011-2012 riots, regional language politics). Inequality & segregation by neighborhood/class. Migrants often live in segregated enclaves. Individual experiences vary dramatically by religion, language, class. Generational: youth more cosmopolitan."}
{:trait/id :trait-mumbai-english-lingua-franca, :trait/city [:city/id "IND-mumbai"], :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "English as business/education lingua franca; code-switching normal", :trait/description "Mumbai's business, tech, media, & education sectors operate in English. English is the language of upward mobility, corporate jobs, tech startups. Code-switching between English-Hindi-Marathi is constant. Speaking English signals education & professional status. Regional language speakers often feel marginalized.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to rural/regional India: Hindi or local language dominant. English is foreign/elite signal.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Kachru (1988) 'World Englishes'; Mohanty (2010) 'India as Multilingual Laboratory'; India business anthropology", :trait/caveat "English-only speakers are minority; most Mumbaikars speak 2–3 languages. Marathi language politics (regional pride, resistance to English/Hindi dominance) active. Older generation, uneducated populations, regional language activists resist English hegemony. Digital media diversifying language use."}
{:trait/id :trait-mumbai-inequality-visible, :trait/city [:city/id "IND-mumbai"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "IND"], :trait/title "Stark, visible inequality: penthouse-to-favela proximity", :trait/description "Mumbai's geography embodies extreme inequality: luxury high-rises adjacent to sprawling slums (favelas). Informality is massive (60%+ of workforce). Class consciousness is acute; daily navigation of class difference. Wealth display and poverty visibility both extreme.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to rural India: poverty predominates; wealth less visible or concentrated. Urban inequality is spatially intense.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Roy (2011) 'City Requiem, Calcutta'; Sharma (2000) 'Poverty, Politics & Housing'; urban sociology", :trait/caveat "Inequality is not monolithic: old money, new tech wealth, migrant workers all navigate differently. Gender, caste, religion intersect with class (Dalit workers face class+caste discrimination). Informal economy is often self-organized, resistant community. Individual agency & solidarity strong despite inequality."}
{:trait/id :trait-zaf-ubuntu-communal, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "ZAF"], :trait/title "Ubuntu philosophy: collective identity, communal responsibility", :trait/description "South African culture emphasizes 'ubuntu' (I am because we are). Collective well-being is prioritized; individual success is contextual to community. Truth & Reconciliation Commission legacy shapes ongoing emphasis on communal healing.", :trait/contrast "NOT lack of individual agency; individuals are embedded in relational networks.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Louw (2010) 'Ubuntu & Care of Souls'; Shutte (1993) 'Philosophy for Africa'; TRC (1995–2002)", :trait/caveat "Post-apartheid rhetoric idealizes ubuntu; actual behavior varies by class, race, region. Tension between ubuntu and individualism in urban professional contexts. Generational difference: younger urbanites may prioritize individualism. Language group affects philosophy (Xhosa, Zulu, Sotho have distinct ubuntu framings)."}
{:trait/id :trait-zaf-time-polychronic, :trait/kind :time-perception, :trait/region [:region/id "ZAF"], :trait/title "Time as flexible, relationship-centered (African time)", :trait/description "South African time culture prioritizes relationships and present-moment engagement over strict scheduling. 'African time' (polychronic) reflects colonial resistance and relational values. Punctuality is less rigid than Western norms; flexibility is adaptive.", :trait/contrast "NOT disorganization; reflects prioritization of human connection over clock time.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hall (1976) 'Beyond Culture'; Hofstede IVR; business anthropology on South Africa", :trait/caveat "Corporate sectors (especially multinationals) increasingly adopt monochronic norms. Urban/rural divide: rural areas maintain stronger polychronic culture. Class matters: corporate professionals may operate on different time culture than service sector. Generational shift occurring in younger cohorts."}
{:trait/id :trait-zaf-reconciliation-culture, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "ZAF"], :trait/title "Reconciliation & restorative justice, legacy of Truth & Reconciliation Commission", :trait/description "South Africa's post-apartheid identity is shaped by TRC (1995–2002), which emphasized truth-telling and communal healing over punitive justice. This creates cultural emphasis on dialogue, accountability, and forward-looking reconciliation rather than retribution.", :trait/contrast "NOT erasure of harm; reconciliation model seeks justice through acknowledgment and restoration.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "TRC Final Report (1998); Tutu (1999) 'No Future Without Forgiveness'; post-apartheid sociology", :trait/caveat "Reconciliation process was incomplete; many communities remain unhealed. Racial tensions persist; younger Black South Africans critique TRC for insufficient reparations. Justice vs. reconciliation remains contested. Individual variation by race, class, language, urban/rural context is substantial."}
{:trait/id :trait-zaf-oral-tradition, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "ZAF"], :trait/title "Oral tradition, storytelling, communal knowledge transmission", :trait/description "South African culture values oral storytelling, narrative, and communal knowledge-sharing. History is transmitted through stories, proverbs, and collective memory. Formal meetings and gatherings often emphasize narrative context and communal voice.", :trait/contrast "NOT lack of written skill; written tradition also exists, but oral medium remains culturally central.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Ong (1982) 'Orality & Literacy'; anthropological research on African communication", :trait/caveat "Urban, educated populations proficient in written media. Language diversity (11 official languages) affects how orality is practiced. Generational shift toward digital communication ongoing. Individual variation by language, education, region high."}
{:trait/id :trait-dubai-expatriate-majority, :trait/city [:city/id "AE-dubai"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "AE"], :trait/title "Expatriate-majority culture: transient identity, diversity normalization", :trait/description "Dubai's population is ~85% expatriate (Indian, Filipino, Pakistani, Western, Arab). This creates normalized cultural fluidity, code-switching, and temporary identity. Community is transient; relationships are often short-term and instrumental. Diversity is economic norm, not ideological commitment.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to rural UAE: Emirati-dominant, stable kinship networks, long-term community ties.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Eissa (2010) 'UAE Labor Migration'; anthropology of Gulf expatriate culture; Sassen (2001) 'Global City'", :trait/caveat "Expatriate hierarchies exist: Western expats vs South Asian workers face different treatment. Class and nationality dramatically affect experience. Emirati citizens occupy top positions; power dynamics obscure 'diversity'. Legal restrictions on UAE citizenship create precarity for expats. Generational: newer arrivals vs decade-long residents have different integration levels."}
{:trait/id :trait-dubai-hierarchical-respect, :trait/city [:city/id "AE-dubai"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "AE"], :trait/title "Hierarchical respect: Emirati authority, formal protocols, status differentiation", :trait/description "Dubai corporate and official culture emphasizes formal hierarchy, deference to Emirati leadership, and respect for status. Titles matter; formal protocols are observed. Business relationships involve clear rank differentiation. Authority is not questioned directly; indirect approaches are preferred.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to Western egalitarianism: hierarchy is explicit and expected.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede Power Distance; business culture research on Gulf states; Eissa (2010)", :trait/caveat "Expatriate-majority demographics create informal hierarchies that undermine official protocols. Western expats sometimes resist formal hierarchy; South Asian workers often navigate strict power distance. Generational: younger Emiratis more Western-influenced. Gender hierarchies persist (women's autonomy varies by nationality, class, family structure)."}
{:trait/id :trait-dubai-tech-forward-identity, :trait/city [:city/id "AE-dubai"], :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "AE"], :trait/title "Tech adoption, AI-forward mentality, modernization-obsessed identity", :trait/description "Dubai's cultural identity is intertwined with technological modernity and future-orientation. AI adoption, smart city initiatives, and tech investment are sources of pride and national branding. Tech innovation signals progress; resistance to tech is viewed as backward.", :trait/contrast "CONTRAST to Bedouin heritage: tradition, oral knowledge, minimal tech dependency.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Observation; Wiedmann et al. (2007) 'Creative destruction in Dubai'; smart city branding", :trait/caveat "Tech adoption is top-down policy, not necessarily grassroots cultural embrace. Older Emiratis may retain traditional values. Expatriate tech workers (Indian, Filipino) have different relationships to tech than Emirati citizens. Actual AI implementation gaps exist despite branding rhetoric. Digital divide by class/nationality present."}
{:trait/id :trait-chl-dignity-equality-consciousness, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "CHL"], :trait/title "Dignity & equality consciousness post-Pinochet", :trait/description "Chile (1973-1990 dictatorship aftermath) values dignity, equality, & social justice. Protest culture strong (2019 uprisings). Inequality stark; upper vs lower-class divide deep. Identity forged through resistance & democratization.", :trait/contrast "NOT utopian; Chile pragmatic about persistent inequality.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Pew Research LAM, Chilean sociology", :trait/caveat "Urban Santiago more activist; rural more conservative. Class deeply affects consciousness. Gen Z more radical; older generation cautious post-trauma. Wealthy insulated from inequality experience."}
{:trait/id :trait-per-collectivism-indigenous-roots, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "PER"], :trait/title "Collective responsibility & indigenous heritage integration", :trait/description "Peru balances indigenous (Quechua, Aymara) & Spanish colonial heritage. Communal decision-making valued (ayllu tradition). Family loyalty & collective identity strong. Yet economic inequality shapes access to tradition.", :trait/contrast "NOT romanticization; indigenous communities experience discrimination & economic marginalization.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Shweder, Peruvian anthropology", :trait/caveat "Urban Lima cosmopolitan/Spanish-identified; highlands Quechua-dominant. Class affects indigenous language maintenance. Younger generation code-switching between Spanish & indigenous identity."}
{:trait/id :trait-ven-resilience-humor-amid-collapse, :trait/kind :emotional-expression, :trait/region [:region/id "VEN"], :trait/title "Dark humor & resilience amid economic collapse & migration trauma", :trait/description "Venezuela (post-2013 economic crisis, 7M+ emigrated) shows resilience through dark humor, music, & community. Yet collective trauma, hunger, medical collapse shape emotional baseline. Diaspora scattered globally.", :trait/contrast "NOT erasure of suffering; humor is survival mechanism.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede, Venezuelan diaspora sociology", :trait/caveat "Trauma deeply affects perspective; crisis generation marked by loss. Emigrated vs stayed populations diverge in experience & hope. Class structured crisis access (wealthy fled; poor remained). Recovery timeline uncertain."}
{:trait/id :trait-rus-powerdistance-vertical-authority, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "RUS"], :trait/title "High power distance & vertical authority hierarchy", :trait/description "Russian culture emphasizes strong authority, centralized hierarchy, & respect for power. Leadership from above normative (tsarist legacy → Soviet → Putin). Formal protocols, deference to superiors central. Skeptical of egalitarianism.", :trait/contrast "NOT authoritarianism; hierarchy seen as coordination necessity.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede (strong data), GLOBE Study", :trait/caveat "Urban Moscow/St. Petersburg younger generation more egalitarian. Rural/older strongly hierarchical. Putin regime intensifying verticality post-2022. Dissidents/LGBTQ+ experience oppression. Class affects authority experience."}
{:trait/id :trait-rus-stoicism-endurance-fatalism, :trait/kind :emotional-expression, :trait/region [:region/id "RUS"], :trait/title "Stoicism, endurance, & fatalistic worldview", :trait/description "Russian culture valorizes suffering endurance, stoic resilience, & acceptance of hardship (dostoevskian tradition). Fate (sud'ba) seen as external force. Literature, philosophy emphasize existential struggle. Yet strong social bonds within hardship.", :trait/contrast "NOT hopelessness; stoicism coexists with community.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Russian cultural studies, literature", :trait/caveat "Urban Moscow professionals less fatalistic. Rural/Soviet-generation more stoic. Contemporary war (Ukraine) reshaping emotional baseline. Emigrated Russians vary widely in outlook."}
{:trait/id :trait-kaz-nomadic-hospitality-soviet-legacy, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "KAZ"], :trait/title "Nomadic hospitality traditions & Soviet institutional legacy", :trait/description "Kazakhstan blends nomadic Turkic hospitality (guest as sacred) with Soviet collectivism (institutional obedience). Elder respect combined with 70-year communal identity. Oil wealth created new urban/rural divide post-independence.", :trait/contrast "NOT contradiction; both traditions coexist.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede, GLOBE Study, Central Asian anthropology", :trait/caveat "Astana (capital) vs Almaty vs rural steppes vastly different. Younger generation (post-1991) less Soviet-identified. Russian/Kazakh language divide affects identity. Oil wealth concentrated; rural poverty persists."}
{:trait/id :trait-kaz-ethnic-diversity-Russian-minority-tension, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "KAZ"], :trait/title "Ethnic diversity with Kazakh-Russian tension", :trait/description "Kazakhstan 68% Kazakh, 19% Russian (post-Soviet). Language politics (Kazakh revival post-1991) create tension. Kazakhization policies marginalize Russians/minorities. Yet pragmatic coexistence in business/urban centers.", :trait/contrast "NOT assimilation; minorities maintain distinct identity.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Pew Research, Central Asian studies", :trait/caveat "Almaty (historically Russian) more cosmopolitan. Younger generation more nationalist Kazakh. Russian speakers facing language pressure. Class wealth cuts across ethnicity."}
{:trait/id :trait-aus-egalitarianism-mateship, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "AUS"], :trait/title "Egalitarianism, mateship & tall-poppy syndrome", :trait/description "Australian culture values equality, informality, & 'mateship' (camaraderie). Tall-poppy syndrome: high achievers pulled down culturally. Distrust of authority, irreverent humor. Yet Aboriginal marginalization contradicts egalitarian myth.", :trait/contrast "NOT absence of hierarchy; egalitarianism masks power imbalances.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Pew Research, Australian cultural studies", :trait/caveat "Aboriginal & Indigenous Australians experience structural exclusion. Urban/educated more egalitarian; rural/working-class preserve status. Class & ethnicity shape equality experience. Post-colonial awareness rising in younger Gen Z."}
{:trait/id :trait-nzl-bicultural-maori-european-identity, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "NZL"], :trait/title "Bicultural (Māori-European) identity & indigenous rights focus", :trait/description "New Zealand officially bicultural: Māori (indigenous) & Pākehā (European). Māori language revival, Treaty of Waitangi acknowledgment central. Yet structural Māori inequality persists. Urban Pākehā often less engaged with Māori culture than rural.", :trait/contrast "NOT post-racial; active decolonization in progress.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Pew Research, Māori studies, NZ sociology", :trait/caveat "Pākehā awareness varies widely. Māori face ongoing discrimination & inequality. Treaty settlements partial. Younger generation more bicultural-conscious. Rural/urban divide on Māori engagement."}
{:trait/id :trait-cub-resilience-isolation-revolution-legacy, :trait/kind :emotional-expression, :trait/region [:region/id "CUB"], :trait/title "Resilience & humor amid US embargo & revolutionary isolation", :trait/description "Cuba (US embargo 1962-present, Soviet collapse 1991) shows resilience through music, dance, humor. Hardship & material scarcity coexist with cultural vitality. Revolution loyalty complex: younger generation questions ideology. Isolation creates distinct cultural identity.", :trait/contrast "NOT idealization; material hardship is real.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede LAM region, Cuban cultural studies", :trait/caveat "Post-1991 special period trauma shapes perspective. Generational divide: older embrace revolution; youth ambivalent/frustrated. Emigrated vs island populations diverge vastly. Class affects embargo experience (wealthy access contraband; poor suffer)."}
{:trait/id :trait-jam-caribbean-orality-creole-english-identity, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "JAM"], :trait/title "Caribbean orality, Patois/Creole language, & post-colonial identity", :trait/description "Jamaica values oral tradition, storytelling, Jamaican Patois (Creole). English colonial legacy coexists with linguistic pride in Patois. Music (reggae, dancehall) central to expression. Identity hybrid: African heritage + Caribbean + British colonial.", :trait/contrast "NOT dialect inadequacy; Patois is sophisticated language system.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Hofstede, Caribbean linguistics", :trait/caveat "Class determines English/Patois fluency (upper-class speak English, poor Patois). Education system privileges English. Generational: youth increasingly Patois-proud. Urban Kingston vs rural parishes differ. Diaspora (USA/UK) navigate language switching."}
{:trait/id :trait-uzb-soviet-legacy-family-honor, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "UZB"], :trait/title "Soviet institutional legacy & traditional family honor codes", :trait/description "Uzbekistan (former Soviet republic, 1991-present independence) blends Soviet collectivism with Turkic/Islamic family honor systems. Mahalla (neighborhood council) traditional authority coexists with state. Extended family loyalty paramount. Yet authoritarian government controls dissent.", :trait/contrast "NOT contradiction; both systems function simultaneously.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "GLOBE Study, Central Asian studies", :trait/caveat "Urban Tashkent cosmopolitan; rural Ferghana Valley traditional. Younger generation less Soviet-identified. Gender roles increasingly contested (women's workforce participation rising). Authoritarian control limits open expression."}
{:trait/id :trait-kyr-nomadic-hospitality-herding-culture-mountain-identity, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "KYR"], :trait/title "Nomadic herding culture & mountain independence identity", :trait/description "Kyrgyzstan (high-altitude, nomadic heritage) values hospitality, independence, mountain pride. Herding traditions shape time perception (seasonal rhythm). Soviet period disrupted but didn't erase nomadic identity. Mountain geography creates isolation & self-reliance.", :trait/contrast "NOT primitivism; nomadic traditions adaptive & sophisticated.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Central Asian anthropology, GLOBE", :trait/caveat "Urban Bishkek vs remote mountain yurts vastly different. Younger generation urbanizing & losing herding knowledge. Economic hardship (post-Soviet) reshaping livelihoods. Gender: women herders play major role but status contested."}
{:trait/id :trait-svk-cultural-identity-tension, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "SVK"], :trait/title "Cultural identity tension: Czech vs Slovak distinction post-1993", :trait/description "Slovakia declared independence 1993 after Czechoslovak split. Slovaks emphasize national distinctiveness vs Czech dominance. Slovak language & culture pride strong. Tension between Western (EU) orientation & Slavic heritage.", :trait/contrast "NOT hostility; pragmatic nation-building with identity assertion.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede, GLOBE Study, Slovak national institutes", :trait/caveat "Younger generation less defined by Czech split. Urban Bratislava more cosmopolitan. Rural/older emphasize Slovak distinctiveness. Regional variation (West more EU-connected, East more conservative)."}
{:trait/id :trait-svk-family-collectivism-catholicism, :trait/kind :value-system, :trait/region [:region/id "SVK"], :trait/title "Family collectivism & Catholic values", :trait/description "Slovakia predominantly Catholic (60%+). Family loyalty & collective decision-making normative. Religion shapes moral framework & time perception (holy days, fasting). Extended family networks important.", :trait/contrast "NOT rigid; younger generation secularizing.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Pew Research EU, Hofstede", :trait/caveat "Urban youth increasingly secular. Rural/older strongly religious. Regional variation: East (Kosice region) more traditional Catholic. Class affects religiosity."}
{:trait/id :trait-rom-hospitality-warmth-gypsy-tension, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "ROM"], :trait/title "Warmth & hospitality balanced with Romani marginalization", :trait/description "Romania known for strong hospitality & warmth. Yet Romani population (8-10%) faces discrimination, poverty, social exclusion. Tension between Romanian hospitality norms & systemic marginalization. Class & ethnicity deeply intersected.", :trait/contrast "NOT uniformity; hospitality often limited to in-group.", :trait/confidence :high, :trait/source "Pew Research EU, anthropological studies on Romani", :trait/caveat "Urban Bucharest more cosmopolitan. Rural more traditional & exclusionary. Younger generation more progressive on Romani inclusion, but historical prejudice persists. Class strongly affects inclusion patterns."}
{:trait/id :trait-rom-oral-tradition-storytelling, :trait/kind :communication-style, :trait/region [:region/id "ROM"], :trait/title "Oral tradition & storytelling cultural value", :trait/description "Romanian culture values oral tradition, narrative, & storytelling. Poetry, folk music, & historical narratives central to identity. Written communication sometimes less valued than spoken relationship-building.", :trait/contrast "NOT illiteracy; deep literary tradition alongside oral preference.", :trait/confidence :medium, :trait/source "Hofstede, Romanian literary/cultural studies", :trait/caveat "Urban professionals increasingly written-focused. Rural maintains stronger oral tradition. Generational shift: youth embrace written digital communication."}
